CAT = "phletCivic"; SEG = "CIV"
Q = [
("A historian reads a diary written by a soldier during the Philippine-American War. What kind of source is it?",
 "Primary source", ["Secondary source", "Tertiary source", "Oral tradition"],
 "A primary source was produced by a witness or participant at the time. A textbook chapter about the war would be a secondary source."),
("Checking whether a document is authentic, for example by testing its paper and ink, is called:",
 "External criticism", ["Internal criticism", "Historiography", "Content analysis"],
 "External criticism tests a source's authenticity. Internal criticism weighs the credibility and meaning of what the source says."),
("Who founded the Katipunan in 1892?",
 "Andres Bonifacio", ["Emilio Aguinaldo", "Apolinario Mabini", "Marcelo H. del Pilar"],
 "Andres Bonifacio and his companions founded the Katipunan in Tondo in July 1892, days after Rizal's La Liga Filipina was broken up by his arrest."),
("Where was Philippine independence proclaimed on June 12, 1898?",
 "Kawit, Cavite", ["Malolos, Bulacan", "Biak-na-Bato, Bulacan", "Tejeros, Cavite"],
 "Emilio Aguinaldo proclaimed independence from his house in Kawit, Cavite. Malolos later hosted the congress of the First Republic."),
("Which group of American teachers arrived in 1901 to help set up public schools in the Philippines?",
 "The Thomasites", ["The Peace Corps", "The Ilustrados", "The Propagandists"],
 "The Thomasites were named after the USAT Thomas, the ship that brought a large group of them in 1901 as the public school system was set up."),
("Which 1934 United States law set a ten-year transition to Philippine independence under a Commonwealth?",
 "Tydings-McDuffie Act", ["Jones Law", "Philippine Organic Act", "Bell Trade Act"],
 "The Tydings-McDuffie Act created the Commonwealth, inaugurated in 1935, with independence after ten years. The Jones Law of 1916 only promised it."),
("The Spanish Educational Decree of 1863 is remembered for providing:",
 "Free primary schooling in each town", ["The first state university", "English as the medium of teaching", "Free college for all young natives"],
 "The decree ordered at least one primary school for boys and one for girls in each town, free for the poor, and a normal school for teachers."),
("In which year did the People Power Revolution on EDSA take place?",
 "1986", ["1972", "1983", "1992"],
 "The peaceful EDSA uprising of February 1986 ended the Marcos regime. 1972 was the declaration of martial law and 1983 the assassination of Benigno Aquino Jr."),
("Where was Jose Rizal born?",
 "Calamba, Laguna", ["Dapitan, Zamboanga", "Kawit, Cavite", "Malolos, Bulacan"],
 "Rizal was born in Calamba, Laguna, on June 19, 1861. Dapitan was his place of exile from 1892 to 1896."),
("To whom did Rizal dedicate El Filibusterismo?",
 "The three priests Gomburza", ["His mother, Teodora Alonso", "The Filipino youth", "His fiancee, Leonor Rivera"],
 "El Filibusterismo (1891) is dedicated to Fathers Gomez, Burgos and Zamora, executed in 1872. The Noli was dedicated to the Motherland."),
("Which law requires courses on the life, works and writings of Rizal in Philippine schools?",
 "Republic Act 1425", ["Republic Act 7836", "Republic Act 10533", "Republic Act 4670"],
 "RA 1425 of 1956, the Rizal Law, requires the study of Rizal's life and works, especially the Noli and the Fili. RA 7836 is the teachers' law."),
("In Noli Me Tangere, which character returns from Europe hoping to build a school in San Diego?",
 "Crisostomo Ibarra", ["Elias the boatman", "Padre Damaso", "Kapitan Tiago"],
 "Ibarra comes home from Europe and plans a school for the town, a plan the friars sabotage. Elias is the boatman who helps him."),
("In which essay did Rizal argue that the so-called laziness of Filipinos had causes in colonial rule?",
 "On the Indolence of the Filipinos", ["The Philippines a Century Hence", "To the Young Women of Malolos", "Memoirs of a Student of Manila"],
 "In The Indolence of the Filipinos (1890) Rizal traced the problem to climate, wars, forced labor and colonial policies, not to race."),
("Rizal's letter \"To the Young Women of Malolos\" praised them for:",
 "Seeking a school to learn Spanish", ["Joining the Katipunan in Bulacan", "Writing for La Solidaridad abroad", "Founding a Malolos newspaper"],
 "Twenty young women of Malolos petitioned to open a night school to learn Spanish. Rizal praised their courage and urged women's education."),
("What was La Liga Filipina, founded by Rizal in 1892?",
 "A civic group for peaceful reform", ["A secret revolutionary army", "A reform newspaper in Barcelona", "A school for girls in Manila"],
 "La Liga Filipina sought unity and reform by peaceful means. It ended when Rizal was arrested and exiled to Dapitan days after its founding."),
("Whose history did Rizal annotate to show that Filipinos had a culture before Spanish rule?",
 "Antonio de Morga", ["Ferdinand Magellan", "Miguel Lopez de Legazpi", "Antonio Pigafetta"],
 "Rizal annotated Morga's Sucesos de las Islas Filipinas (1609) to show the advanced society that existed before colonization. Pigafetta chronicled Magellan's voyage."),
("Which ethical theory judges an act right if it produces the greatest happiness for the greatest number?",
 "Utilitarianism", ["Deontology", "Virtue ethics", "Natural law theory"],
 "Utilitarianism, associated with Bentham and Mill, judges acts by their consequences. Deontology judges acts by duty, whatever the result."),
("Kant's categorical imperative tells us to act only on rules that:",
 "We could will as universal laws", ["Make the most people happy", "Our own culture already accepts", "Serve our own self-interest best"],
 "Kant's test asks whether the maxim of an action could be a law for everyone. Maximizing happiness is the utilitarian test."),
("According to Aristotle, a virtue such as courage is:",
 "A mean between two extremes", ["Following the law exactly", "Obeying one's feelings", "Doing whatever brings pleasure"],
 "Aristotle's golden mean places courage between cowardice (deficiency) and recklessness (excess). Virtue is built by habit."),
("A situation in which a person must choose between two actions, each backed by a moral reason, is a:",
 "Moral dilemma", ["Moral relativism", "Moral fallacy", "Moral standard"],
 "A moral dilemma forces a choice between competing obligations, where honoring one means failing the other."),
("The view that right and wrong depend only on what a given culture approves is called:",
 "Cultural relativism", ["Ethical egoism", "Divine command theory", "Universalism"],
 "Cultural relativism holds that moral standards are set by each culture. Universalism holds that some standards apply to everyone."),
("Which Filipino value refers to a lifelong sense of gratitude owed to someone who helped you?",
 "Utang na loob", ["Pakikisama", "Bahala na", "Amor propio"],
 "Utang na loob is a debt of gratitude. Pakikisama is getting along with the group, and hiya is a sense of shame or propriety."),
("Which thinker held that moral law is grounded in human nature and reason, as created by God?",
 "Thomas Aquinas", ["John Stuart Mill", "Jeremy Bentham", "Friedrich Nietzsche"],
 "Aquinas developed natural law theory: by reason people can know the good built into human nature. Mill and Bentham are utilitarians."),
("A teacher keeps her promise to a pupil even though breaking it would be more convenient. Which ethical view best explains her choice?",
 "Duty-based ethics", ["Ethical egoism", "Hedonism", "Cultural relativism"],
 "Duty-based (deontological) ethics says some acts, like keeping promises, are required whatever their convenience or result."),
]
